s staff quit on him. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le10ob07aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "disorganized"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "not properly planned and controlled"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:  It was such a disorganized mess that most of the chefs quit. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le10ob07aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to confide"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to communicate information in private with the expectation that it will not be repeated "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Bill confided in me that he didn't want to run the place anymore. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le10ob07aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to turn things around"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to change something so that it becomes successful"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You were able to turn things around that quickly?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le10ob07aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to tread water "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "maintain an upright position in deep water by moving the feet with a walking movement and the hands with a downward circular motion"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: No, we treaded water for about six months."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn11le10ob07aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Cause and Effect: So/Such ... That + Clause"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When you want to talk about a remarkable situation (one that is extraordinary or unusual), you can use the structure So/Such ... That + Clause."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">So</span> and <span class=\"italic\">such</span> are degree intensifiers (emphasizers) that combine with other modifiers to express a remarkable situation, which is followed by that + clause that expresses an effect.</br ></br >   For example:</br >   <span class=\"italic\">They earned so much money that they could go on a trip around the world.</span> </br ></br >    The cause is:<span class=\"italic\"> They earned a lot of money.</span></br >  The effect is: <span class=\"italic\">They could go on a trip around the world.</span> </br ></br >    You use such before an adjective + noun.</br ></br >    For example:</br >  <span class=\"italic\"> They earned such a large amount of money that they could go on a trip around the world.</span> "}, {"type": "example", "content": "That bite of your cake was so amazing that I decided to get one of my own.  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Bill was willing to spend some money so that we could hire some hot young chefs. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "He made so much money that he decided to open his own restaurant.    "}, {"type": "example", "content": "He created such turmoil that half of his staff quit on him.  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "It was such a disorganized mess that most of the chefs quit.  "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Past Continuous vs. The Simple Past"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the Simple Past in a narrative to talk about a single, complete past event. You use the Past Continuous tense to talk about the background situation that existed at the time."}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example:</br >  <span class=\"italic\">Mike dropped his wallet while he was getting into the taxi.</span></br > <span class=\"italic\">She was shaking with fear as she left the party.</span></br ></br >  The one-time event might have interrupted the situation, or have happened while the situation was in progress."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Several people were busily cooking, some were preparing food, some weren't doing anything, the chef was yelling, and customers were waiting to be helped."}, {"type": "example", "content": "One customer was yelling at a server and waving his hands. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "He lacked the insight and the tenacity needed to make things work in the restaurant business."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Others were complaining to each other about the bad service."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Bill and his partners didn't know anything about running a restaurant.  "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "With a partner, create a job you need to fill."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a list of all the skills you want applicants to have. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Include descriptions of job duties."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Interview classmates for the position in a role play."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Ask questions about the applicant's previous experience."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Ask questions about what tasks they were doing in their past position."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I make a list of skills needed for a particular job listing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I use Past Continuous tense to explain prior job duties. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Use so and such as intensifiers to describe your previous duties. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I practice interviewing questions. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I give feedback on how to interview for a job."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "img_path": "", "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can talk about relationships, character traits, beliefs and opinions."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "